# Runtime dependencies — the direct set, stated by a human.
#
# This file is the input; `requirements.txt` is COMPILED from it by pip-compile and
# must not be hand-edited. The lock carries the full transitive closure, which is what
# makes an install reproducible. Regenerate BOTH locks together:
#
#   docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/w" -w /w python:3.12-slim sh -c \
#     "pip install pip-tools && \
#      pip-compile --no-strip-extras --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in && \
#      pip-compile --no-strip-extras --output-file=requirements-dev.txt requirements-dev.in"
#
# `--no-strip-extras` is not optional: without it `psycopg[binary]` compiles down to
# a bare `psycopg`, and the image installs a psycopg with no libpq behind it.
#
# Compile on Linux. pip-compile resolves for the platform it runs on, and the
# torch/nvidia wheels sentence-transformers pulls in differ on Windows and macOS —
# a lock compiled there would not describe the image CI and Docker actually build.
#
# No `--generate-hashes`, deliberately. It was tried: on this dependency set one
# compile ran past thirty minutes and pulled more than 30 GB, because hashing forces
# a download of every candidate the resolver considers rather than metadata alone.
# Without it the same compile takes under three minutes. A lock that costs half an
# hour to regenerate is a lock nobody regenerates — which is exactly how Chapter 11's
# lock file went stale. Transitive pinning is what OPS-1 asked for and it is intact;
# hashes would add tamper-evidence on top, and can be revisited if the resolution
# ever gets cheap enough for Dependabot to redo it on every bump.
#
# Direct dependencies stay pinned with `==` here, not ranged. A range does not just
# group badly under Dependabot, it hides what you run (LESSONS_LEARNED Ch. 11).
#
# Test-only tooling lives in `requirements-dev.in` and is deliberately absent from
# the production image.

